IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cma-types`
IMPORT `cma-definitions`

-- Computer Misuse Act 1993 — GOAL 2: IS AN OFFENCE MADE OUT? (ss 3-10, 12)
--
--   "On these facts, are the elements of the alleged offence present, and is
--    no exception in the section met?"
--
-- What the court finds (knowledge, purpose, intent, a presumption rebutted)
-- is an input. What the section says follows from those findings is a rule.
-- Source: ../source/CMA1993.txt lines 159-540.

§ `Goal 2 — the offences`

§§ `The text encoded (FORK F1)`

-- The Act was last amended by Act 21 of 2025 with effect from 30 December
-- 2025 (scam offences, caning, s 8A(1) "subject to subsection (5)", s 8B(5)).
-- The source prints only the text as amended; for conduct before that day the
-- text that applied is not in it, so the encoding refuses (NOTES, fork F1).
`the text is in force from` MEANS YMD 2025 12 30

@ref Legislative History item 15 (Act 21 of 2025)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the encoded text governs conduct on` d IF
    d AT LEAST `the text is in force from`

§§ `The elements of each offence`

-- s 3(1): "any person who knowingly causes a computer to perform any function
-- for the purpose of securing access without authority to any program or data
-- held in any computer". s 3(3): not directed at any particular program, data
-- or computer is immaterial.
-- s 4(1)-(2): "causes a computer to perform any function for the purpose of
-- securing access to any program or data ... with intent to commit an offence
-- to which this section applies"; it applies to "an offence involving property,
-- fraud, dishonesty or which causes bodily harm and which is punishable on
-- conviction with imprisonment for a term of not less than 2 years". s 4(4):
-- authorised or unauthorised access, and when the intended offence is
-- committed, are immaterial.
-- s 5(1): "does any act which the person knows will cause an unauthorised
-- modification of the contents of any computer"; s 5(4): permanent or temporary
-- is immaterial.
-- s 6(1): "knowingly — (a) secures access without authority to any computer for
-- the purpose of obtaining ... any computer service; (b) intercepts ... without
-- authority ... any function of a computer by means of an electromagnetic,
-- acoustic, mechanical or other device; or (c) uses ... the computer or any
-- other device for the purpose of committing an offence under paragraph (a) or
-- (b)".
-- s 7(1): "knowingly and without authority or lawful excuse — (a) interferes
-- with, or interrupts or obstructs the lawful use of, a computer; or (b) impedes
-- or prevents access to, or impairs the usefulness or effectiveness of, any
-- program or data stored in a computer".
-- s 8(1): "knowingly and without authority, discloses any password, access code
-- or any other means of gaining access ... if the person did so — (a) for any
-- wrongful gain; (b) for any unlawful purpose; or (c) knowing that it is likely
-- to cause wrongful loss to any person".
-- s 8A: see the rule below. s 8B, s 9: see `a dealing is an offence`.
-- s 10(1): "(a) obtains or retains any item to which this section applies —
-- (i) intending to use it to commit, or facilitate the commission of, an
-- offence under section 3, 4, 5, 6 or 7; or (ii) with a view to it being
-- supplied or made available ... for use in committing ... any of those
-- offences; or (b) makes, supplies, offers to supply or makes available ... any
-- item to which this section applies, intending it to be used to commit ...".
@ref ss 3(1), (3); 4(1), (2), (4); 5(1), (3), (4); 6(1), (3); 7(1); 8(1); 8A(1)-(4); 8B(1)-(4); 9(1)-(4), (7); 10(1)-(2)
GIVEN o IS AN `Alleged offence`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the elements are made out` o IF
    CONSIDER o
    WHEN `s 3 unauthorised access to computer material` knowingly purpose effect auth THEN
            knowingly AND purpose
        AND `secures access to a program or data` effect
        AND `without authority` auth
    WHEN `s 4 access with intent to commit an offence` caused effect intended THEN
            caused
        AND `secures access to a program or data` effect
        AND CONSIDER intended
            WHEN JUST i  THEN `an offence to which s 4 applies` i
            WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN `s 5 unauthorised modification of computer material` knows effect auth THEN
            knows
        AND `a modification of the contents of a computer` effect
        AND `without authority` auth
    WHEN `s 6 unauthorised use or interception of computer service` knowingly a b c THEN
        knowingly AND (a OR b OR c)
    WHEN `s 7 unauthorised obstruction of use of computer` knowingly a b THEN
        knowingly AND (a OR b)
    WHEN `s 8 unauthorised disclosure of access code` disclosed a b c THEN
        disclosed AND (a OR b OR c)
    WHEN `s 8A disclosure in relation to the national digital identity service` user disclosed knew presumed lawful scam THEN
        `s 8A(1) is made out` user disclosed knew presumed lawful
    WHEN `s 8B dealing in another person's credential` credential facts scam THEN
        credential AND `a dealing is an offence` facts
    WHEN `s 9 dealing in personal information obtained by a computer offence` knew personal facts THEN
        knew AND personal AND `a dealing is an offence` facts
    WHEN `s 10 obtaining items for use in certain offences` item ai aii b THEN
        item AND (ai OR aii OR b)

-- s 4(2).
@ref s 4(2)
GIVEN i IS AN `Intended offence`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an offence to which s 4 applies` i IF
        i's `involves property, fraud or dishonesty, or causes bodily harm`
    AND i's `maximum imprisonment on conviction, in years` AT LEAST 2

-- s 8A(1): a user who discloses "any password or access code of the user in
-- relation to the national digital identity service, or provides any other
-- means of securing access in the identity of the user" and "does so knowing,
-- or having reasonable grounds to believe, that the purpose ... is for any
-- person to commit, or to facilitate the commission by any person of, any
-- offence under any written law".
-- s 8A(3): the user "is presumed, until the contrary is proved, to have
-- reasonable grounds to believe" that purpose if (a) the act is "for any gain";
-- (b) "knowing that it is likely to cause wrongful loss"; or (c) the user
-- "fails to take reasonable steps to ascertain the identity and physical
-- location of the person" to whom it is disclosed.
-- s 8A(4): "It is not an offence ... if the user ... had reasonable grounds to
-- believe that the purpose ... is to use or access the national digital
-- identity service to carry out a transaction in the identity of the user for
-- a lawful purpose."
-- s 8A(2) eases proof (no proof the purpose was carried out, of the specific
-- offence, or of a specific recipient); it adds no element.
@ref s 8A(1)-(4)
GIVEN user IS A BOOLEAN, disclosed IS A BOOLEAN, knew IS A BOOLEAN, presumed IS AN `s 8A presumption facts`, lawful IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 8A(1) is made out` user disclosed knew presumed lawful IF
        user
    AND disclosed
    AND (knew OR `the s 8A(3) presumption stands` presumed)
    AND NOT lawful

@ref s 8A(3)
GIVEN p IS AN `s 8A presumption facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 8A(3) presumption stands` p IF
        (   p's `did the act for any gain, wrongful or not, realised or not, to anyone`
         OR p's `did it knowing it is likely to cause wrongful loss`
         OR p's `failed to take reasonable steps to ascertain the identity and physical location of the recipient`)
    AND NOT p's `the contrary is proved`

-- ss 8B(2)-(4) and 9(2)-(4), which are worded alike.
-- (2): "It is not an offence under subsection (1)(a) if the person obtained or
-- retained [it] for a purpose that is not any of the following purposes: (a)
-- for use in committing, or in facilitating the commission of, any offence ...;
-- (b) for the supply or transmission of, or making available ... [it] to be
-- used in committing ... any offence".
-- (3): "It is not an offence under subsection (1)(b) if — (a) the person did
-- the act for a purpose other than for [it] to be used in committing ... any
-- offence ...; and (b) the person did not know or have reason to believe that
-- [it] will be or is likely to be used to commit ... any offence".
-- (4): a person "does not transmit or make available [it] merely because the
-- person provides, or operates facilities for network access, or provides
-- services relating to, or provides connections for, the transmission or
-- routing of data".
@ref ss 8B(1)-(4); 9(1)-(4)
GIVEN f IS A `Dealing facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a dealing is an offence` f IF
    CONSIDER f's `dealing`
    WHEN `(a) obtains or retains` THEN
            f's `purpose: use in committing or facilitating an offence`
         OR f's `purpose: supply or making available for use in an offence`
    WHEN `(b) supplies, offers to supply, transmits or makes available` THEN
            NOT f's `merely provides network access, routing or connection services`
        AND (   f's `purpose: use in committing or facilitating an offence`
             OR f's `knew or had reason to believe it will be or is likely to be used for an offence`)

§§ `Abetment, attempt and preparation (s 12)`

-- s 12(1): one who "abets the commission of or who attempts to commit or does
-- any act preparatory to or in furtherance of the commission of any offence
-- under this Act shall be guilty of that offence and shall be liable on
-- conviction to the punishment provided for the offence". s 12(2): "it is
-- immaterial where the act in question took place". So the participation
-- changes nothing the encoding computes; it is answered here so a caller can
-- see the rule.
@ref s 12(1)
GIVEN p IS A Participation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is guilty of the offence itself, with its punishment` p IF TRUE

§§ `The offence's name`

@ref ss 3-10
GIVEN o IS AN `Alleged offence`
GIVETH A STRING
`the provision of` o MEANS
    CONSIDER o
    WHEN `s 3 unauthorised access to computer material` a b c d                                  THEN "s 3"
    WHEN `s 4 access with intent to commit an offence` a b c                                     THEN "s 4"
    WHEN `s 5 unauthorised modification of computer material` a b c                             THEN "s 5"
    WHEN `s 6 unauthorised use or interception of computer service` a b c d                     THEN "s 6"
    WHEN `s 7 unauthorised obstruction of use of computer` a b c                                THEN "s 7"
    WHEN `s 8 unauthorised disclosure of access code` a b c d                                   THEN "s 8"
    WHEN `s 8A disclosure in relation to the national digital identity service` a b c d e f     THEN "s 8A"
    WHEN `s 8B dealing in another person's credential` a f s                                    THEN "s 8B"
    WHEN `s 9 dealing in personal information obtained by a computer offence` a b f             THEN "s 9"
    WHEN `s 10 obtaining items for use in certain offences` a b c d                             THEN "s 10"
